    {"t": "chapter", "num": "1", "title": "Understanding AuraSense in plain English",
     "sub": "What problem it addresses, who it is for, what a first-time user sees, and what it honestly cannot do."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "1. What exactly is AuraSense?"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "AuraSense is a web application that studies the body's signals and estimates which of three laboratory conditions a recording most resembles. Those conditions are neutral (calm baseline), stress (a laboratory stress task) and amusement (a positive-affect film clip). It shows the estimate with a confidence-like score, an uncertainty value, a signal-quality score and an explanation of which measured features influenced the result."},
    {"t": "p", "text": "It is a pattern-similarity tool, not a stress meter. If a recording looks like the stress examples the model learned, AuraSense calls it a \u201cstress-associated pattern\u201d. It never says \u201cyou are stressed\u201d, because physiology alone cannot establish that."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "2\u20133. What real-world problem does it address, and why is it useful?"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Wearables produce large amounts of ECG, skin-conductance and movement data, but raw numbers are hard to interpret. AuraSense demonstrates an end-to-end research workflow: collect or upload signals, check whether they are usable, extract interpretable features, compare them with a published research dataset, show the result honestly, and let a researcher inspect the evidence behind the model. It is useful as a teaching and research prototype because it makes the whole pipeline visible instead of hiding it behind a single percentage."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "4\u20136. Intended users, main objective and major capabilities"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "The application has three roles. An individual user explores their own readings, uploads files, builds a personal baseline and downloads reports. A researcher can additionally run model experiments, see the Research Centre and generate research reports. An institution/admin account can see consent-based cohort aggregates. The main objective is to demonstrate a scientifically cautious, explainable stress-pattern pipeline, not to diagnose anyone."},
    {"t": "b", "items": [
        "A public landing page that reports the real measured balanced accuracy from the model artifact.",
        "A personal workspace with an overview, latest estimate, quality state, trends and analysis history.",
        "A simulated live monitor with five scenarios, waveform views, heart-rate estimation and prediction history.",
        "AI Analysis on cached WESAD reference windows and on demonstration datasets.",
        "BioSignal Lab for real CSV/TSV uploads with automatic channel and sampling-rate detection.",
        "A personal baseline built from the user's own completed analyses, with z-score comparison and reliability tiers.",
        "A Research Centre with model comparison, confusion matrices, per-class and per-participant results, importance and rerunnable experiments.",
        "Privacy-limited Institution Insights with weekly aggregates, minimum-cohort suppression, CSV export and a PDF.",
        "Reports & History with PDF generation, download and deletion, plus account settings, JSON export and account deletion.",
    ]},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "7. What happens when a new user opens the application?"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "They land on a public page that explains the product and shows the real model metric. They can create an individual account, or a researcher account with the invitation code AURA-RESEARCH. After signing in they reach /app, which loads their analyses, baseline, sessions and model information from the API. With a fresh account all lists are empty: the interface explains how to start with a simulated session or a CSV upload. The demonstration accounts already contain seeded examples so a faculty demonstration does not begin with empty screens."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "8\u20139. How can people use it, and in how many ways?"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "There are four practical paths. First, watch a simulated session and see how a signal becomes an estimate. Second, open a cached WESAD window for a chosen participant and condition to see genuine model inference on research data. Third, upload a real CSV from any device that can export time-series channels. Fourth, use the Research Centre to inspect evaluation evidence or rerun an experiment. All four paths end in the same stored analysis format, so results can be compared in history, baseline and reports."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "A concrete example"},
    {"t": "p", "text": "Suppose a student, Meera, has a chest-strap recording exported as CSV with columns time_s, ECG, EDA and Resp. She opens BioSignal Lab, drops the file and optionally enters the sampling rate. AuraSense recognises the time column, calculates 250 Hz from the timestamps, forms 60-second windows every 30 seconds, measures heart rate, HRV, skin-conductance responses, breathing rate and signal quality, then runs the gradient-boosting model. If the file is only 48 seconds long, it forms one shorter window and says so. The result might be \u201cneutral-associated pattern, 99% confidence, quality 100/100\u201d. She can open the analysis, read which features pushed the result, generate a PDF, and later compare the reading with her baseline. At no point does the app claim to know whether she is stressed; it reports similarity and limitations."},

    {"t": "h2", "text": "10. Current limitations a student must be able to state"},
    {"t": "b", "items": [
        "WESAD has only 15 participants, recorded in a laboratory with a fixed protocol. It does not represent everyday life or all bodies.",
        "The model is trained on chest sensors, while many consumer devices provide wrist PPG. Uploaded wrist data may not be comparable.",
        "There are three classes, and amusement is the weakest: recall is about 53%, so it is often confused with neutral.",
        "Probabilities are not calibrated. A score of 100% means the model is confident, not that it is correct 100% of the time.",
        "The uncertainty number is normalised entropy, not a statistical confidence interval.",
        "Live Monitor and demonstration recordings are synthetic, even though they pass through the real model.",
        "Signal units are assumed, not converted. Uploading ECG in volts instead of millivolts, for example, changes feature magnitudes.",
        "There is no hardware integration, no medical validation and no clinical claim.",
        "The deployment is local only; security and data-retention hardening are incomplete.",
    ]},

